Design an iterator that supports the peek operation on an existing iterator in addition to the hasNext and the next operations.

Implement the PeekingIterator class:

PeekingIterator(Iterator<int> nums) Initializes the object with the given integer iterator iterator.
int next() Returns the next element in the array and moves the pointer to the next element.
boolean hasNext() Returns true if there are still elements in the array.
int peek() Returns the next element in the array without moving the pointer.
Note: Each language may have a different implementation of the constructor and Iterator, but they all support the int next() and boolean hasNext() functions.

import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

class PeekingIterator implements Iterator<Integer> {
    private Iterator<Integer> iterator;
    private Integer nextElement = null;
    private boolean hasNextElement = false;

    public PeekingIterator(Iterator<Integer> iterator) {
        this.iterator = iterator;
        // Advance once at the beginning to cache the first element
        advanceIterator();
    }
    
    // Returns the next element in the iteration without advancing the iterator.
    public Integer peek() {
        if (!hasNextElement) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException();
        }
        return nextElement;
    }
    
    @Override
    public Integer next() {
        if (!hasNextElement) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException();
        }
        // Save the current cached element to return
        Integer result = nextElement;
        // Move the underlying iterator forward to cache the next value
        advanceIterator();
        return result;
    }
    
    @Override
    public boolean hasNext() {
        return hasNextElement;
    }

    // Helper method to look ahead and update the cache
    private void advanceIterator() {
        if (iterator.hasNext()) {
            nextElement = iterator.next();
            hasNextElement = true;
        } else {
            nextElement = null;
            hasNextElement = false;
        }
    }
}
